{
  "dom_id": "step-q328327",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$",
